I recently used the CSLG distribution media to upgrade a number of packages
on my system, including fortran. One of the update packages was for the
fortran runtime libraries. I later noticed that the old libraries were still
around, so I used setld -d to delete them. Well, lo and behold, I
subsequently got link and run time errors on fortran programs that had
compiled and run before. After a bit of diagnostic work, I determined that
"link" looks at a limited set of directories for the libraries. Evidently,
there had been symbolic links between those directories and the fortran
libraries. Using setld -d delted them, too, so the libraries couldn't be
found. BUT, why couldn't the new libraries be found? Because there were no
links! The CSLG install script apparantly transfered the subset from the
distribution media, installed it using setld, but didn't put links to the
libraries into the directories that "link" searches.

So, my question: 
1) Am I correct in my belief that the CSLG install script is not completely
configuring a package for use? (Otherwise, there would have been links to
the locations of the new libraries). The release notes on the CSLG media
made no notice that any sort of links or post-installation tasks were required.

2) I installed pascal and C++ at the same time. Am I missing links for those
libraries, too?

3) Is there some sort of documentation that describes where files/links
should be placed for a given package to operate? Or must I shell out big
$$'s and purchase the DEC manuals?

Ken


