In article <1732F11C93S85.LOPEZJ@vm.ci.uv.es>, LOPEZJ@vm.ci.uv.es (Jose Agustin Lopez Bueno) writes:
>From: LOPEZJ@vm.ci.uv.es (Jose Agustin Lopez Bueno)
>Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc
>Subject: I need more CPU
>
>Hello, all.
> 
>  I am making a program in Ansi C on Alpha OSF/1 with 32 Mb RAM.
>This program is a simulation of biological populations and I need
>the most memory and % of processor. This system is used only by me.
>But when I launch big programs (many memory) and the system swap to
>disk, my % of CPU is minimized (96 % --->  4 %) (I check this with
>syd, a system monitor program). How could I get more % of CPU? I probed
>with nice, but dont work much.
> 
> 
>  Thanks you very much for your time�
>  Agustin
>
Easy:
	0) nice won't do anything. If you're the only user and paging, 
it is useless.

	1) increase the amount of memory (I don't mean VM, but real RAM).
You are seeing an execess of paging which is what degrades your CPU
performance: it is waiting for the disk all the time.

	2) Program a little better. Use locality of reference to
ensure that you are accessing your memory in an efficient way, so
that you can reduce the need for continuous paging. e.g.:

	int my_array[15000][15000];

	for (i = 0; i < 10000; i++)
		for (j = 0; j < 10000; j++)
			my_array[i][j] = compute_something();
is different from
	for (i = 0; i < 10000; i++)
		for (j = 0; j < 10000; j++)
			my_array[j][i] = compute_something();
                                 ^  ^

Depending on how your compiler allocates the arrays, you see a lot
of swapping with one method and very little with the other. If I 
remember it well, the first one was better for C.

Look for the properties of your computation that allow you to reduce
paging, for example if each element computation is independent:

	int my_array[BIGSIZE];

	for (i = 0; i < NUM_ITERATIONS; i++)
		process_the_entire_array(my_array);

is completelly different from
	for (i = 0; i < BIGNUM; i++)
		do_all_iterations_on_element(&my_array[i]);
or say,
		do_all_iterations_on_a_secion_of_the_array(my_array,
			beg, end);

which would do if an element's computation depends on a limited
set of neighbours...

And more, if your arrays are sparse, you can select better algorithms
or special representations that will require much less memory.

And so on...

If your program can't be optimized in any way to use locality of
reference, then go for more RAM memory. But it would be a strange
program if you can't rearrange it.

	3) Consider running simulations with less elements if
possible and later combining the results of the partial simulations.

	4) If everything else fails go for an account in a computer
with more memory, or in one of the supercomputers of CICA, Santiago, 
or the Annella in Catalonia. They'll surely be glad to help you.


