In article <D1trMI.DDG@ncgrafix.demon.co.uk>, mark@ncgrafix.demon.co.uk (Mark Wassell) writes:

|>What I'm trying to do is to write some code that prints a calling trace of a 
|>running program on demand. Using the ld* man pages I have managed to
|>come up with something that converts a pc into a function and that can skip 
|>back through the stack to print a trace. However, if there are functions from
|>shared libraries in the way then it fails (as pc>function and frame 
|>information won't be stored in the executable file). 

DECladebug does exactly this.  If you are running on OSF V3.0, simply
attach to the running process, hit control-C thereby temporarily stopping
it, and then ask DECladebug 'where'.  Instant stack trace.  You may have
to load shared libraries along the way, but why re-invent the wheel?
It's probably unsafe to try and do the stack trace on a *running* program
anyway, since while you are figuring out the stack trace, it will be 
changing.


