Steve "Stevers!" Coile <scoile@osf1.gmu.edu> wrote:
>Bernd "Bernie" Meyer (root@wombat.hanse.de) wrote:
>>scoile@osf1.gmu.edu (Steve "Stevers!" Coile) writes:
>[...]
>>And what about the wonderful behaviour that "dir *.*" shows all files while
>>"dir *" shows only those without extension, but "copy *.* b:" copies only
>>files with extension and "copy *" copies all (or some such nonsense, I never
>>really made statistics).
>
>Incorrect.  You haven't done *any* research, let alone "made statistics".
>Please try again.
>
>"DIR *.*" will generate a listing of all files in the current directory.
>"COPY *.* B:" will copy all files in the current directory to the default
>directory of the B: drive.  In both cases, files which have the "hidden"
>bit set will be excluded.

However, he was right in that "DIR *" and "COPY *" are inconsistent.
He got it backwards though; "DIR *" shows everything regardless of extension,
while "COPY *" and "DEL *" work only for files with no extension.
This seems much safer than the other way around, i.e. if you do "DIR *"
followed by "DEL *" you'll delete fewer files than were listed, not more.

My own pet peeve is that "dir *A.*A" is identical to "dir *.*".
