|> >path in some internal buffer only, and other programs rely on ending the
|> >tmp path with a '\'.
|> 
|> Those are all flaws with the programs themselves, not DOS.

These are flaws that were introduced by the anarchy that DOS introduced.
Every Unix (except turnkey systems) comes with a /usr/include, /tmp, /bin,
/etc directory structure and a set of libraries, notably libc.a (libs and
header files and various utilities are missing in turnkey systems).
There is also a native C compiler and assembler, perhaps not portable among
different platforms, but suitable to compile stuff for the particular system.
This avoids the problem that every hacker company invents their own object
file format, library structure and header layout, for instance, and leads to
a considerably constant and reliable platform.

In DOS the only constant factor is that there are config.sys and autoexec.bat
in the root directory of the drive. There is even no convention where to
put the native DOS executables (attrib, join, tree, edlin, and that stuff)
to; some have it under \DOS, \PCDOS, \SYSTEM, whatever. I have even seen
systems where they were put in the root directory and eventually the genius 
who did that came up saying that the system refused to add more programs.

I have never seen this with Unix. Some well-known structuring of the software,
and well-defined environment variables (TERM, HOME, USER, PRINTER, PWD, SHELL)
have their advantage. Nota bene: there is no TMP environment variable in Unix.
Guess why. SHELL is a different issue with DOS. There is no publicly known
agreement when to use SHELL and when COMSPEC, so you waste valuable environment
space to define both, because programs use them inconsistently (check out the
DOS native utilities for some incompatibilities there).

It is a cheap complaint to say it's the programmers who produced shitty programs
that don't follow some conventions, if there are none, and even the OS writer
cared the hell about structure. We had this debate already with the use of
wildcards, and anyone except you agreed there is a discrepancy.

|> 
|> >                     Almost every compiler claims the environment variables
|> >LIB and INCLUDE for itself, making it almost impossible to have more than
|> >one compiler properly installed.
|> 
|> Short sightedness on the part of the compiler developers, not DOS.  Are
|> you telling me you can have multiple definitions of LIB and INCLUDE
|> in your Unix environment?  I don't think so.
There are no LIB and INCLUDE environment variables in Unix. There are, however,
well-known and well-defined conventions for a system where stuff belongs to.
Example: System libraries belong in /lib (sometimes it is a link to /usr/lib
actually), X11 stuff can usually found in /usr/bin/X11, /usr/lib/X11,
/usr/include/X11, but even if it is elsewhere for instance for disk space
reasons, there are links to the actual location. And if you recompile a GCC
for Unix and put it in /usr/local/bin, it is *itself* that knows where its
header and library files are for the particular system.

|> 
|> How does Gcc handle LIB and INCLUDE in DOS?
GCC_INCLUDE, GCC_LIB, or GNU_LIB, GNU_INCLUDE, or LIB, INCLUDE, or GCC_DIR,
or GCC_FILES; I have seen any useful and unusable combination with gcc, as well
as with other compilers.

