README.1ST? Can I dare to engage my *add-your-preferred-wordprocessor* to
load this file? Which version of unpacker does my FOO.ZIP require? Is it
still compatible with the old pkzip 1.1 I have? And this nifty FOO.C_ that is
supposed to be a compressed example from the MS-C compiler, which expand shall
I use to unpack it? The expand.exe from Windows doesn't work. I know of at least
4 incompatible versions of expand.exe over the time (but probably you haven't
encountered so many, unless you bought various MS products in the past).
And have you an idea what FOOBAR.DLL is? Yes, the extension says it is a DLL,
but for Windows, or DOS, or relic from the last attempt to install OS/2 or WinNT,
and which the hell utility does this DLL belong to? Convention is to stuff
everything into \windows or \windows\system nowadays, which I would not
consider a really clever way of dealing with software packages.

|> >                          Every program that even just does a fart,
|> >claims space in the PATH variable which is an extremely limited resource.
|> 
|> True, but that's arrogance on the developers' parts, and it can be
|> avoided if you refuse to allow the installation program take control
|> of your system (I, myself, prefer to make the choices myself so that
|> I know how my system is set-up).
You and I surely know, but millions of DOS users are not even knowledged
enough to understand the structure of config.sys and autoexec.bat,
and are helpless, if the program that has just been installed won't run,
since the maximum path length has been exceeded and the newly added directory
won't be searched. Even more, Windows has the bad manner to add itself to
the front of the path, and thus shifts the directories at the end of the 
path variable outside the recognized area. You get Windows, but obscurely
lose the access to other utilities that formerly worked.
Again it is a lame excuse to condemn the programmers, they cannot behave better
that the OS allows or forces them to. Why is the path length limited? Why
is there a restriction in the length of the remainder of a DEVICE= line in
config.sys? Why should any programmer care about good structuring if the
OS is a cardhouse and the OS programmers wrote the config.sys parser with
char line[80]; while (...) { gets(line); do_something(line); } (provided 
stdin is redirected to config.sys, just to demonstrate the insecurity of gets
vs. fgets).

|> 
|> >|> >UNIX provides security measures to prevent one user from intervening another,
|> >|> >by password protection to prevent unauthorized access to user accounts, by
|> >|> >storing attributes to limit access to user (and system) files, and by 
|> >|> >memory protection to prevent user processes from intervening one another.
|> >|> 
|> >|> All to deal with issues that never come up in a DOS environment.
|> >These issues simply apply if you think of exporting file systems through
|> >Windows for Workgroups, for instance. 
|> 
|> WfWg handles all that.  DOS doesn't need to because with WfWg, it *can't*.
|> What's your point here?
Perhaps it exports a directory securely (securely is at most readonly) to a 
different host, but if I want to do damage, I do it locally. Everyone can
use the attrib command in conjunction with del to cause the maximum amount of
damage. Under Unix, attrib=chmod and del=rm, but the important difference is,
that chmod will refuse to touch any file that does not belong to me. Likewise,
if you export a file to some host with WfWg for writing, than any weenie on
the other side of the net connection has control to it as there is no way to
find out who is there at the remote PC. This is the slight difference for
NFS, for instance.

And again, the "user" factor applies here. You need to be knowledged enough
to do the OS stuff that could improve the situation (like management of
exporting things, restructuring config.sys and the path, etc.). Users are
people who want to do some real thing with their PC, not fight against
the OS. You cannot expect them to understand and circumvent the problems of
a half-baken OS (one should have expected that DOS would have become cooked
over the decade it exists, but it just became a museum of incompatible, outdated,
half-hearted patches, that will need to be patched again by additional software
like WfWg. Recently I heard this museum will be mainly renamed to Win95
(or Win96, I don't remember) and gets another layer of paint on its surface.

|> >They apply even more severe, if the PC is accessible - and must be -
|> >by several persons. If you mainly think of a PC for home use, it is not
|> >so much an issue unless you have clever children who understand how
|> >they can remove your whole textprocessing software in order to acquire the
|> >necessary 10MB diskspace to install DOOM.
|> 
|> If you have kids willing to do that then you have more problems than
|> just inadequate operating system security measures.
It just serves as an example, where it would be useful to have some more
protection. But users don't even think of such an improvement as they have
been told for a decade that they don't need it in DOS. It is not children's
behaviour that is to be discussed here.
 
|> Besides, how many *home* users do you think are going to use a password
|> to guard their machine *at home*?
The point is: does DOS have such a facility at all in its kernel or supplied
code? Or do you have to buy some additional software (as a most simple level
a screen saver)? And then: "Daddy, what is the screen saver password, we
want to play pacman?" (or we will use the big red button if the system
is locked).

