They also built an AT/370 board set (I own one! - "one from the vaults!")
It ran a version of VM (Virtual Machine) 370 CMS (Conversational Monitor
System - or Cambridge Monitor System depending upon which side you take in
the war! :)  I really works, runs REAL S/370 object code and uses a set of
three special M/C68000 processors to implement the instruction set.  It was
a bit of a hybrid in that it switched to PC mode for I/O but other than 
that was pretty much a real, multi-tasking, virtual storage operating system...
Unfortunately, it had a very short lifespan since the marketeers figured
that anyone who would run VM was obviously a mainframe candidate and could
be expected to put out a quarter-million dollars for a base-level machine.
Such (IMHO) stupid thinking succeeded in killing off VM/CMS which (again,
IMHO) was IBM's only viable alternative to UNIX in winning the interactive
computing wars... and their ultimate answer?  -- AIX!!  (barf!)

And it was, sadly, very slow.  A pity.  And they later came out with a 
VM offering that could be used with (of course) a micro-channel PS/2 front-end
for I/O, etc. and was priced starting around $20K if I remember correctly...
far outside the realm of the average hacker!

