In article <3flv4r$k22@m1.cs.man.ac.uk> fellowsd@cs.man.ac.uk (Donal K. Fellows) writes:

   The one thing I like about DOS that isn't available as such under UNIX
   is the ability to create programs that can't be displayed using DIR
   (unless you give an extra command line option), and yet which don't
   have to be mentioned in the path since you have to change to the right
   directory to use them. I can supply examples of this sort of thing
   (namely, my great compressing/uncompressing \GAMES directory scheme :)
   to anyone who is really interested.

How is this different from:

mkdir d
cp prog1 d/.prog1
cp prog2 d/.prog2

After all, these files (.prog1 and .prog2) aren't listed by `ls'
unless you give a special option (`-a'); and "they don't have to be
added to your path, because you have to be in the right directory."
(That latter sounds like a restriction, not a feature; I can't figure
out how it's *good*.)

Anyway, in Unix you can do things like what gzip's `gzexe' does.  It
turns an executable into a shell script that skips the first N lines,
runs the rest through gunzip, and then executes it.  This works
cleanly because of Unix's `#!' execution feature.  You can't do this
in DOS.  

(Of course, in GNU, you can just gzip the executable normally without
any further ado, because the execserver knows how to uncompress
itself.  Can't do that in DOS either.)

