Two points here.

 1. The file name globbing in vanilla DOS is highly inconsistent, and
    will only match characters relative to the start of a filename /
    extension. I have programmed a workround for this in the past
    which provided more unix-like filename globbing, but I think that
    I may have mislaid the source... :(

 2. Why would you want to put symlinks or application directories
    directly in the root of your filing system anyway? This is a
    broken concept from the start anyway! I think that it is better to
    place apps / data files / etc. in directories that make a
    consistent logical sense (eg. C source in \SOURCE\C\FOO\ under DOS
    or /usr/local/src/c/foo under UNIX (if it was a system-wide
    source) or in $HOME/src/c/foo if it was just something I was
    tinkering with). Then if I wound that I was frequently changing to
    the directory, I'd either make a symlink to it from $HOME (under
    UNIX) or I'd put a batch file containing the command to change
    into the directory in somewhere like \UTILS\BATCH (under DOS).

The one thing I like about DOS that isn't available as such under UNIX
is the ability to create programs that can't be displayed using DIR
(unless you give an extra command line option), and yet which don't
have to be mentioned in the path since you have to change to the right
directory to use them. I can supply examples of this sort of thing
(namely, my great compressing/uncompressing \GAMES directory scheme :)
to anyone who is really interested.

Donal.
