> From the literature I've read, it seems that a DCE client can make a
> non-blocking RPC call.  What I haven't been able to find out is
> whether a call-back mechanism is used or if the client programmer is
> expected to make use of DCE threads to achieve the parallelism?
> Please send responses to kurr@ctron.com.

	I believe the non-blocking call you are referring to uses the
"maybe" semantics of RPC.  With a "maybe" call the sender posts a
one-way RPC (message, really) to a receiver.  The "RPC" returns when the
call has been posted -- not delivered or executed, just successfully
transmitted to the network.  There are no return parameters on a "maybe"
RPC so there will be no callback; nor does the programmer have to read
the results from a futures structure or anything.  Hope this helps.  Regards,

