>these two pieces of software so successful, they shouldn't be lecturing
>on design!

DOS is so well distributed because once it became popular, Microsoft
began requiring that resellers distribute it exclusively, quashing
competition.  The U.S. federal government has recently cracked down
on that.  Before that, it was about the only operating system out
there for Intel-based systems.

Unix is well distributed because in its early days, it was distributed
for free.  Even today, Linux and several other versions of Unix are
freely available to all takers.

So, the two most popular operating systems in existance are in their
position because their users were effectively forced to accept it, or
because it was free, and anything free is going to get somewhere.
Neither got where they are through fair, competetive means in a level
playing field.

-Steve Coile


