Ross Morley (ross@prpa.philips.com) wrote:
[...]
>Oh, that reminds me. Wildcards. DOS screws them up too. DOS *doesn't*
>do wildcards, each individual application program has to process them,
>hence you get inconsistencies in the way "*" and "?" are handled in
>difference command lines. In UNIX, the command interpreter (shell) 
>handles the wildcards so the apps don't have to worry about them and
>you get consistency.

I've never had a problem with inconsitancies with the handling of
DOS wildcards.

The advantage in not expanding wildcards is that the length of the
command line buffer is not a factor in what will be passed to the
program.  You also don't have to worry about the maximum size of
argv.  You also don't have to worry that an opportunely named file
may alter the behavior of your command.

-Steve Coile


