>Tell me that next time your girlfriend accidentally wipes out 
>your favorite program or you discover a virus has attached itself
>to command.com and scrambled your FAT table....

DOS is not intended to deal with multiple users.  It assumes that
either only one user will have access to the system, or that multiple
users won't mess with something that isn't specifically theirs.  Unix
doesn't protect you from yourself, or anyone else that has access to
your account.  DOS doesn't either (think of DOS as being a system with
only one account).

As far as viruses go, if someone were to write a virus, it could
coneivably gain access via a superuser.  Unfortunately, under DOS, one
is always running under what is effectively superuser mode.

-Steve Coile


