>>can't do this sort of thing.

I recognize this thread for what it is.. pure flame bait.. but I can't
sit on my hands and let an incorrect statement like this go by
unattended.  Expansion is a major strength of sh-programming.. and so
is parameter execution.  You really need to know that sort of stuff to
get the most from UNIX.

A UNIX user will opt to use a sh-command line like:

   for f in *.doc; do cp $f `basename $f .doc`.bak; done

or, if the list of files is small enough, a sh-command line like:

   for f in a b and so forth; do cp $f.doc $f.bak; done



Do you need a script for your UNIX account?  Is that what you're
really asking for?  Well, this one should work:

#!/bin/sh
## cpx: copies a set of files with the same ext. to a different ext.
## example: to copy all *.doc files to *.bak -- cpx doc bak
for f in *.$1; do cp $f `basename $f .$1`.$2; done


