I'm not familiar with "rip", but if all you want is something to
communicate with, you're actually better off purchasing a single,
multi-user system and a bunch of terminals than you are buying ever
user their own PC.  Why?  Terminals are cheaper and you have the
benefit of centralized management.

>                                            Stop trying to task the system
>to do something it's really not designed to do, and it'll be almost trouble
>free, as well. 

Beginning with the Intel 80386, all chips used by PC's are inteded to
be used to provide virtual memory and virtual machine services, else
the necessary support wouldn't have been built into the chip (and we
probably wouldn't have ever moved beyond the 286 if not for those
capabilities).  That *DOS* doesn't use those capabilities does not
mean the systems aren't meant for it.  Remember, *Intel* was the
one that put the capability in the chip.  *Microsoft* didn't change
DOS to take advantage of it.

