cdlevin (cdlevin@shadow.net) wrote:
>   Dos has features that were patterned after UNIX? Name them, and tell 
>me why you think they're corrupted.

As many people have already pointed out, pipes (|) and redirection (>
and <).  The DOS MKDIR and RMDIR commands are derived from their Unix
counterparts.  MORE was originally a Unix utility.  Filters of any sort
were all originally Unix.  Wildcards are derived from the Unix command
shell syntax.

>                                    Also, UNIX was designed to run on a 
>mainframe then,

Unix was originally designed as a *single-user* system.  Multiuser
capabilities were added later.

>                about compatible with a workstation today, wile dos was 
>strictly designed for the PC.

However, most modern PC's are where workstations were several years ago.
DOS has not kept pace.

>                              SO the real question would be, can a 
>mainframe do more than a PC? You guessed it.

Yes.  No PC in the world can keep pace with the IO a worthwhile mainframe
can produce.  No PC is ever going to have the expandability.

>   Dos is probably more user friendly, and the bulk of the problems with 
>it that I hear about are either A) switchology  or B) overtaxing the system.

How do you define "overtaxing"?  The bulk of the problems I have with
DOS is that it has rediculous memory constraints (requiring a detailed
understanding of how memory is layed-out and managed in order to 
reconfigure so that one can use many programs) and can't do more than
one thing at a time, even when that one task does not consume all of
the processing capabilities the CPU has to offer.  The CPU is *capable*
of doing more, DOS just doesn't take advantage of it.  With the advent
of Windows multi-tasking, DOS's limitations became quite apparent.
Namely, memory protection *doesn't exist*, allowing any program to
interfere with any other program, deliberately or unintentionally.
DOS has not inherent file-sharing capability, which prevents two
processes on the same machine from corrupting one-another's files.

>The 1000 dollar PC wasn't designed to do alot of the things that it's 
>taxed to do,

Incorrect.  The Intel 80386, 486, and Pentium processors are designed
for virtual memory and virtual machine use.

>             and computer manufacturers are hard pressed to keep pace 
>with the current trends, the comparison not unlike spoiled children in a 
>candy store. I want this, I want that! They have to build new machines 
>every year to keep pace with the industry in general.

The vast, vast majority of the demands on PC and PC-peripheral designers
come from DOS users, not others.  Are you complaining about DOS users,
then?

>   Dos is basically plug and play.

No, but *Windows* has made software and hardware installation easier.
Microsoft *had* to do *something* to compete with Apple, since the
Macintosh was stealing customers away *simply because* it was easier
to install, configure, and use new software and hardware.

Installing many programs under DOS, and most hardware, requires a
knowledge of technical details.  Failure to know these technical
details will result in problems.  That's why people have to know
the "tricks" you mentioned in your first posting.

>   Dos users are pretty common.

True.

>   Dos has a better security system. File sharing in UNIX is dangerous.

DOS has *no* security system.  DOS is not designed with multiple users
in mind.  Anyone that uses your machine can access any file on that
machine, and there's nothing you can do to prevent it.  Unix and many
other modern operating systems can be configured to disallow unauthorized
tampering.

File sharing between users in a network is dangerous, yes, but often
useful or even necessary.  If not, why would Microsoft make such
capabilities available in Windows for Workgroups?  File sharing between
processes on a single machine is often necessary.  It's dangerous on
a DOS machine because DOS was not designed to handle it.

>   Properly configured, Dos will run lika a champ. If you're having a 
>problem running it in DOS, then you will probably have a problem running it
>under any OS. The limitations are mostly Hardware.

Wrong.  Memory limitations *alone* rule out a great many possibilities
on DOS platforms that are possible on other systems.

>   Last but not least, Dos is being replaced in 95 by a gui type OS. Wait 
>until that comes out, and see if that doesn't better suit your needs.

Windows 95 will, if it does as advertised, be a *significant* improvement,
and I think will change a lot of people's opinions about Microsoft's
operating systems.

>   By the way, what's the price on UNIX? I've never seen it in stores.

There are several free implementations of Unix.  Commercial versions are
going to cost significantly more than a copy of DOS.

>   Whats the minimum hardware requirement?

As others have mentioned, about that of a machine that will run Windows.

>   Lastly, if UNIX is soooo good, then why doesn't everybody run it.

Because it's cryptic and isn't designed for the average user.  Unix
is and always has been best suited for development work by experienced
computer users.  DOS is used by so many people because Microsoft
required, for a time before the federal government cracked down on 'em,
that stores may only sell DOS if they pre-install it on *every* machine
they sell (or at least to the exclusion of any other operating system).
In other words, if you wanted to sell DOS at all, you had to sell it
every time, and you couldn't make any other operating system as
convenient to the purchaser.

>   I'll tell you why, DOS is a good system, not too complex, and easy to 
>learn.

It is moderate to good at what it was designed to do, which wasn't much.
It certainly isn't very complex, and the basic commands are easy to learn.
Using the machine to its potential is impossible, however, under DOS.

>       ANd it does a fine job of running my little 386, which by the way,
