cdlevin <cdlevin@shadow.net> wrote:

> Dos has features that were patterned after UNIX? Name them, and tell
> me why you think they're corrupted.

First a bit of history: Microsoft's involvement with Unix (XENIX)
predates its acquisition of DOS from Seattle Computer.

The most obvious Unix-like features added to DOS 2.0 include things
like hierarchical directories (See Note 1), I/O redirections, and
choice (through the CONFIG.SYS SHELL command) of command line
processors. There are even more under the hood--in the names for
system calls, for example.

If you want corruption: Sticking with CP/M's slash for command line
options and using the backslash for the directory delimiter. (Not to
worry, the system calls like RENAME, etc. support both.)

Note 1: DOS hierarchical directories' resemblance to Unix is only skin
deep. Underneath, it's the same old CP/M FCB crap mixed with
FATs--i.e., no file permissions, inflexible 8+3 format, no links.

