If I may put my 2 cents worth in......

In reading through the threads on this debate ( which is the SAME
debate that has been going on for years), there are two relativly
simple reasons why DOS/Windows is still the dominate OS of choice.

First- for about 7 years, DOS's 640k conventional RAM limit was not
an issue with mainstream users.  This fact plus its ease of use 
(compared to UNIX) got it firmly established in the business 
community- the largest target for developers.  Once this comfort
level was reached, it has been nearly impossible to disloge it- Who
in their right mind is going to ignore such a large potential for sales?
Developers learned to live with it- and the hardware industry was 
glad to follow suit.

When the 640k limit became a real bother ('89-90), the race was on for
the next OS.  This is when the UNIX community shot themselves in the
foot.  At that time, there were countless flavors available, and more
being added every week!  Only a few isolated programs could run under
more than three different flavors!

UNIX people have formed numerous committees over the years to try and
stabilize the platform, but petty squabbling over whos was best- what
was to be left out- standardization of basic routines and command
structures, etc doomed UNIX to forever being the best OS that few
would ever use!  Even today, with SOME stabilization being actually
being a reality- Berkley UNIX guru's can become lost when confronted
with a System V varient!

Thats when Bill Gates jumped in (and IBM tried to!).  With Windows, 
Microsoft gave both users and developers a relativly painless migration
path from DOS to Multitasking.  Is Windows better?- Hell no!! BUT- MIS
did not have to worry about the wars raging over a *Standard* UNIX.  

Most businesses hate being locked overmuch into a only a few choices
of software vendors.  With Windows, for all its faults, they have a 
wide choice available, no problems on the flavor of the month, and 
great commitments form a large ISV community.

It's interesting, but even after Windows became a big hit, there was 
STILL time for UNIX to get it's act together and present a challange.
But nothing happened UNTIL M/S announced Chicago's specs! Then, all
of a sudden- after almost 10 YEARS(!) of trying- the Open Systems specs
are FINALLY being set.  Great effort guys, but about three years too
late!

So, the discussion on the merits of one over another is pointless.
UNIX *IS* superior in all respects but one.... the number of systems
using it! DOS/Windows rules the desktops because Gates & Co had a 
better vision of the future, superior marketing, the backing of IBM (for
a few years, anyway), and all that gave the MIS directors of the world
a better solution than the uncertanty of who would win the UNIX wars.

hutch

