>Ross Morley <ross@prpa.philips.com> wrote:
>>buytaert@imec.be (Steven Buytaert) writes:
>>>cdlevin (cdlevin@shadow.net) wrote:
>>>:    Dos has features that were patterned after UNIX? Name them, and tell 
>>>: me why you think they're corrupted. 

>>> OK, just to have an apetizer. I know this example has nothing to do with
>>> a kernel on the unix side, but let's say 'pipes' and 'wildcards' ?

[Stealth pedant mode on :-)]

* Pipes - yes, though they're really just temp files and so not 
  quite the same thing.

* Wildcards - no, sorry.  Etymology of DOS' wildcard handling is:
  MSDOS <- CP/M <- DOS 11 (for PDP-11.  Actually, it may not have
  been DOS 11, but it was one of the DEC proprietary OS's).  Some-
  what reduced in functionality, though, I believe: e.g. "A*BB.*"
  is equivalent to "A*.*" in MSDOS whereas DEC OS's handle this
  construct "correctly," though they may not have long ago, I
  can't remember now.  There are other differences relating to
  "?", as well.

* Handle based I/O (i.e., file descriptors, Microsoft called them
  "Xenix compatible" system calls).  Introduced in MSDOS V2.0.
  Before then, MSDOS used File Control Block (FCB) based I/O,
  another concept inherited from CP/M.  To this day, FCB's are
  still supported for the sake of backward compatibility.

* Nested directory structure (with bastardized backslash pathname
  separator because forward slash used for "command line switches"
  (another characteristic patterned after DEC proprietary OS's)).

* Character and block device drivers, though, again, much 
  bastardized and reduced in functionality and boot-time loading
  added.

Can't think of any others at the moment.

[Stealth pedant mode off]

>>Oh, that reminds me. Wildcards. DOS screws them up too. DOS *doesn't*
>>do wildcards, each individual application program has to process them,
>>hence you get inconsistencies in the way "*" and "?" are handled in
>>difference command lines. In UNIX, the command interpreter (shell) 
>>handles the wildcards so the apps don't have to worry about them and
>>you get consistency.

There are system calls for wildcard handling.  Programs which use them
should be consistent, or largely so, though the interpretation is so
limited that nasty surprises will crop up for users accustomed to
something else.

Letting the Unix shell expand wildcards makes for a consistent
interpretation because most Unix programs don't even know there is
such a thing as wildcards; they just see a list of names.  People
accustomed to the MSDOS or Digital interpretationa will encounter
unexpected results.  You can tell the shell not to expand wildcards,
but that is extremely crippling since most Unix programs and all
but a few Unix utilities do not process wildcards at all.

>I am no expert.   I have only just got Linux installed on my PC, and it's
>not a big deal, but I've spent a couple of hours checking thru Linux
>groups so I thought I'd better say something....

>Surely expanding wildcards in the shell is a BAD THING ?

>rm proggy1.c  rm proggy2.c   shorthand rm prog*   happiness
>typo    rm prog *  gets expanded to wipe directory.

>rm cannot check, because it just gets a list of files expanded
>by the shell and does its duty.

Ah.  This is a philosophical dispute and you can never hope for
consensus, but you knew that didn't you?  Just to be contrary, I could
argue that if MSDOS had an rm command, then it would delete prog plus
every file in the current directory without an extension (type).  Of
course COMMAND.COM's "DEL" & "ERASE" commands accept only one
argument, an interpretation I think stinks, personally, but it
wouldn't wipe the directory.  If it were VMS, it would very
pedantically insist that you specify the extension and version number
and so would delete nothing.  Some people may prefer these
interpretations and who's to say they're wrong?  People tend to prefer
what they're used to.  The only thing I, personally, will willingly
defend as a "should" is that the interpretation be consistent and
predictable from command to command.

Regards,
Larry
