>>and so forth). Because the shell handles expansion in UNIX, UNIX
>>can't do this sort of thing.

I have to respond to this, since I seem to have started it.

In this example, the *s mean two completely different things. 
The first is a wildcard that matches the first part of any filename.
The second is a metacharacter which is replaced by whatever the 
wildcard matched.  This is inconsistent use of *, which is confusing. 
In DOS COMMAND.COM, * is used differently by different commands, so 
you just have to remember the idiosyncrasies of each.

Now what if I want to copy all the C and assembler source files and
associated h files and a few specials, but not the object files or
executables, to another directory.
	copy *.c *.s *.h foo.txt bar.txt /some/dir
This will not work in DOS. Instead I have to say
	copy *.c /some/dir
	copy *.s /some/dir
	copy *.h /some/dir
	copy foo.txt bar.txt /some/dir  (or do I have to separate those too?)
In UNIX csh one would write
	cp *.[hsc] {foo,bar}.txt /some/dir
which shows off some more of the powerful wildcard features
available in UNIX (csh in this case).

Somebody else already posted how to rename the extensions in a wad
of files. I use a simple script I wrote for it. One thing about UNIX,
if you can't do something by a direct command, you can easily write
a script. Scripts in DOS (batch files) are too limited to do much
useful - eg. you couldn't write a batch file to do that).

Ross

