> in no way proves that this can't be done right. What's wrong with
> a library routine an application can call that provides a consistent
> user interface and calling sequence? Stratus' VOS did this. Their
> command parser had its share of flaws, but for different reasons.
> And one could write an 'rm' command that was less error prone than
> that of Unix.

Somewhere I have a book of Unix papers that starts off with one
listing "known mistakes" with Unix. The wild card expansion mechanism
and the contortions involved in "quoting" metacharacters figures
prominently in the discussion. The conclusion was, however, most
illuminating: Although hindsight tells us that shared libraries are a
better way to handle wildcards--MKS, for one, does this for their DOS
package--like so many DOS bugs, this mistake is carved in stone, and
it is therefore too late to go back and fix it. (I understand that the
hard part of Windows is maintaining backward compatibility with DOS.)

