|> If you have the TEMP environment variable set, the system should be using
|> it for such temporary files, rather than your current directory.

Of course, there are still some executables that want to have the
environment variable TMP, and there are plenty programs that assume that
TEMP points to c:\tmp, hence they reserve space for 6 characters for the tmp
path in some internal buffer only, and other programs rely on ending the
tmp path with a '\'. Almost every compiler claims the environment variables
LIB and INCLUDE for itself, making it almost impossible to have more than
one compiler properly installed.
There are no naming conventions for files, libraries, directories, so there
is pure anarchy in DOS (unless you spend the time and install everything
properly, and write scripts that locate the real executable and sets up the
environment as required). Every program that even just does a fart,
claims space in the PATH variable which is an extremely limited resource.
The environment buffer is another one, and with network support, you have
no problem at all to exhausted in in the autoexec.bat file already
(4dos is better behaved w.r.t. environment, but it is a secondary product).

|> >UNIX provides security measures to prevent one user from intervening another,
|> >by password protection to prevent unauthorized access to user accounts, by
|> >storing attributes to limit access to user (and system) files, and by 
|> >memory protection to prevent user processes from intervening one another.
|> 
|> All to deal with issues that never come up in a DOS environment.
These issues simply apply if you think of exporting file systems through
Windows for Workgroups, for instance. 
They apply even more severe, if the PC is accessible - and must be -
by several persons. If you mainly think of a PC for home use, it is not
so much an issue unless you have clever children who understand how
they can remove your whole textprocessing software in order to acquire the
necessary 10MB diskspace to install DOOM.
As far as memory protection is concerned: the most used keys with DOS 
were CTRL, ALT, DELETE and the reset button (if everything you do the whole 
day is typing in accounting data into the single program running all the time,
your mileage may vary, but I do programming, and eventually try to find a bug
in software), with *nix (NetBSD) and OS/2 I use now these keys are now
rarely used in combination (ALT-ESC and then closing the offending process
is much more used).

