>Why not try to get dos to tell you all files with the word foo in their
>name. *foo* is _NOT_ going to work. You can't have more than one * before
>and after the . in a DOS wildcard (for common DOS utilities). It's
>ridiculous. And you can't get around that easily.

If anyone cares, there a nice set of utilities out there that goes by
the name of picnix.  It comes with Unix-ish version of many popular
commands, including "ls," "cp," "mv," "cat," "echo" and many others.
They all expand wildcards the way "sh" does.  So, EVEN UNDER DOS:

	ls -l *foo*

works.  So do:

	necho Today is `date` >> logfile
and
	ls -l `which foo.exe`

I seem to recall the author asks $25 for the package, more for source.

There's also a "sh" program for does floating around out there.

