MSDOG was "designed" to be semi-compatible with CP/M and *nothing* else.
UNIX was not designed to run on a mainframe.  you need some serious clues.

>   Dos is probably more user friendly,

false.  MSDOG's command.com is a UI nightmare.  i've taught secretaries
the unix shell, and they *liked it*.  i'll admit they didn't like 'vi'
much, but not many new users do ... it's an aquired taste.

all beside the point since the user herself can manufacturer virtually
any UI she feels comfortable with.  well, if she is using a unix machine
at least.  in MSDOG?  sorry, you must accept only the Bill Approved Interface.

>   Dos is basically plug and play.

false.

>   Dos users are pretty common.

and so are bad drivers on the interstate.

>   Dos has a better security system.

false, since MSDOG has no security system.

>   File sharing in UNIX is dangerous.

absurd.

>   Properly configured, Dos will run lika a champ.

false, unless you like having to reset your computer every few hours,
or reconfigure it to run some other program or environment.

>                                                    If you're having a 
>problem running it in DOS, then you will probably have a problem running it
>under any OS. The limitations are mostly Hardware.

complete hogwash.  i run the full-blown, right from the heart of a
mainframe, version of the NEC-2 antenna analysis program on my Linux
machine -- i had to argue with the fortran->C converter somewhat but
it otherwise runs ace.

on MSDOG?  good screaming luck.  and this is but one example of *many*
programs that are difficult, if not impossible, to run under MSDOG
due to its completely idiotic memory mis-managers and the like.  the DOG
extenders help.  but they are not solutions to the problem, they are
ugly kludges, trying desperately to keep an poorly designed system
viable in an age of very large programs.  the only reason they exist is
that there is an installed base of trillions of MSDOG machines.  period.

