cdlevin <cdlevin@shadow.net> writes:

[...]
>   I only run one thing at a time on my PC, because I can only use one 
>thing at a time on my PC, but I have two communications programs running now
>under windows, and all I have to dpo to get it to perform is to turn off 
>the Macintosh wannabe graphics...
>   Besides that, I am the user, not user"(s)", so basically, if I don't 
>do it here, it won't happen...

This is a very common misconception.  First off, do you REALLY think you
use only one thing at a time?  The primary flaw of DOS (and the Macintosh)
is lack of preemptive multitasking.  Sure, they are single user computers,
but as MILLIONS of users have now discovered, your computer is capable
of doing many things at one time.  The advent of Windows and System 7
has led to users who have word processors, spreadsheets, etc all loaded 
at the same time.  And, you may say you are only using one of those at
a time, but you don't realize how much background processing is going 
on in many of these program in order to make them as fast as possible.
But, all of this is done with very little efficiency.  Huge quantities of
CPU time on your expensive Pentium and PowerPC machines are wasted 
spinning in a wait loop.  

What does preemptive multitasking mean?  It means that the CPU is ALWAYS
doing something.  There will usually be some idle task which will get
CPU cycles when nothing else is available to run, but the CPU never burns
cycles waiting for a disk seek, keypress, etc.  This is one of the main
things that sets the true OS's (Unix, Linux, OS/2, and even Windows NT)
apart from DOS and System 7.  This will be the next revolution in 
personal computers as both Windows and Mac discover preemptive 
multitasking.  Just as the GUI was endlessly criticized as useless,
but is not totally necessary for all but a few backwards holdouts, a 
real OS will become commonplace in PC's in a few more years (both
Windows and Mac are heading that way) and everyone will wonder what they
ever did without them.

>   By the way, all the viruses that permeate the dos environment must 
>have been written on DOS machines, right?...

I imagine so.  I'm not sure what you are getting at except that maybe
you think those are examples of powerful code.  I would not be surprised
to find that DOS was written originally on something like a PDP-11 or
maybe one of those early Intel systems.  So what?

>   Incidentally, I feel alot better knowing that I can use UNIX on the 
>server, and when I'm done, I can pull the plug(disconnect), and I still 
>have my PC, my programs, and I don't have to really worry about anyone 
>else I have to share files with...

Well, this is a dumb arguement.  Unix will always have security
risks since virtually every unix system other than personal home systems
is networked and there's that path to get to the files for the 
determined hacker.  But, do you realize that virtually all business
PC's are now networked as well?  You may pull the plug on your PC, but
I don't know of any ethernet, token ring, etc. users who EVER disconnect
thier machines.  Now, can you argue that security in Novell and WFW networks
(or Appletalk) is so much better?

>   Not to say that DOS is better, but at least when I connect to a 
>multiuser BBS system, I get full color graphics and amazing speed for a 
>little 14.4 modem...

Gee, I guess I don't get full color graphics on my Alpha workstation?
And, you want to talk about amazing speed?

>   If fidonet was networked the way the internet is, then mail would 
>probably be just as fast...

And if DOS was a real operating system the way Unix is, PC's would 
probably be just as fast...


