I'll try to be very brief here, in the hope of imparting a clue or two...

cdlevin <cdlevin@shadow.net> writes:

>   Dos has features that were patterned after UNIX? Name them, and tell 
>me why you think they're corrupted. 

Hierarchical file system (subdirectories, trees). Corrupted because
they use the backslash as the directory separator (commonly used in the
real world as a textual escape for characters that usually have a
special meaning); limit filenames to 8 chars + 3 char extension and no
dots apart from the one before the extension, and almost no other chars
legal (oh how painful it is trying to transfer files to DOS systems!);
use of the extension to control how the OS uses the file (even more
limitation on already limited filenaming possibilities) - UNIX uses
info encoded in the file itself so you can name it *and rename it* as
you wish without changing its meaning. Probably one of the most
annoying limitations of the DOS file system is the lack of symbolic
links.

Command names like mkdir and tee, but they are more limited.

I/O redirection and pipes (except pipes don't work very well because
DOS is single tasking, and I/O redirection doesn't work when invoking
a batch file - you try to redirect the batch file invocation and the 
commands it calls do not redirect their output).

>Also, UNIX was designed to run on a 
>mainframe then, about compatible with a workstation today, wile dos was 

UNIX was designed for mini, much smaller than todays PCs. 'course UNIX
has grown a lot bigger with all the network support and X-windows. So has
DOS/Windows.

>   Dos is probably more user friendly, and the bulk of the problems with 

Nope. It's very very hard, even impossible to do certain things in DOS,
such redirecting output from a batch file, writing useful batch files,
multitasking.

>it that I hear about are either A) switchology  or B) overtaxing the system.

Much of A is due to lack of proper OS support for I/O. 

>   Dos is basically plug and play.

Ahem!!  Can't tell you how much trouble I;ve had trying to "plug and play"
things in DOS.  Interrupt vectors, ports, now TSR conflicts and bizarrely 
screwed up memory mismanagement (lessee, should I load it into extended
memory, high memory, foo memory - what if it conflicts with..) arghhhh!

>   Dos users are pretty common.

Yes. DOS was supplied with IBM PCs from the beginning, so it became the
de-facto standard for PCs (and at the time PCs were too small to run UNIX,
and UNIX doesn't run well off floppies). This allowed Microsoft to gain 
almost a monopoly, and MS and competing vendors alike wrote applications
for MS-DOS. That's why it's pretty common. Nothing to do with quality -
it's almost a monopoly.

But now, enter Linux. Yay!

>   Dos has a better security system. File sharing in UNIX is dangerous.

Dos has a security system? Where?  Oh I get it, DOS achieves network
security by not being connected. Can't beat that for security. But any
user can read or delete any other user's files. DOS programs can even
crash the entire system, messing up other applications.

>   Properly configured, Dos will run lika a champ. If you're having a 

Only if the application you run behavees itself.

>problem running it in DOS, then you will probably have a problem running it
>under any OS. The limitations are mostly Hardware.

Many of the limitations are lack of memory protection, lack of multitasking,
TSR conflicts (TSRs are a workaround for not having memory management and
reentrancy) and hardware conflicts.

>   Last but not least, Dos is being replaced in 95 by a gui type OS. Wait 

Just a flashier Windows. Still won't be multitasking. They'd have to
rewrite the underlying DOS and BIOS to achieve that.

>until that comes out, and see if that doesn't better suit your needs.
>   By the way, what's the price on UNIX? I've never seen it in stores.

It's free. It's called Linux. It's very good, and even runs DOS programs.

>   Lastly, if UNIX is soooo good, then why doesn't everybody run it.

See para about why DOS users are pretty common.

>   I'll tell you why, DOS is a good system, not too complex, and easy to 
>learn. ANd it does a fine job of running my little 386, which by the way,
>is currently running multitasked under windows, with another comm program 

Try an experiment. Run a long compilation, or some other batch job that
generates output as it runs so you can see if it's actually running.
In another window, run an unrelated program (read your email or something).
To read you email you need to click on your email reader to make it the
active window. Observe what happens to your compilation. I think you'll
notice that it stops dead until you once again go back and make it's window
the active one. This is not multitasking. MS has a euphemism for it called
"cooperative multitasking", meaning you can have several tasks loaded at
once and (manually) switch from one to another, but only *one* runs at a 
time. You can't read and reply to email, or edit another file, while waiting
for your compilation.

You mentioned in an earlier post that "DOS has a better help system than
UNIX". No way. I've used DOS help, and despite some hyperlinks, it is 
basically not very informative or easy to get around. The vanilla text-only
UNIX help system (man pages) have *much* more information and better cross
references. When you run a grpahical help interface like the Sun AnswerBook,
you get real good hyperlinks and pictures on-line too. Way better than DOS
or Windows.

I hope you get a chance to try out Linux, or at least get some exposure
to UNIX.

So much for being brief...
Ross

