>>    Dos has features that were patterned after UNIX? Name them, and tell 
Pipes and I/O redirection are borrowed from UNIX.  Moreover, many
DOS INT21 system calls have similar names as their UNIX equivalents,
quoting UNLINK (which is essential a 'DEL' or 'ERASE' in the syntax of
COMMAND.COM) as an example.  

Unlike UNIX, which implements pipes by concurrent processes, DOS
implements pipes using temporary files because it lacks the ability to
multi-task.  The temp. files are always created under the *current*
directly.  This would cause problems when I do a "type
readme.doc|more" in a read-only directory (say on a read-only floppy
or CD-ROM)!


