>>    Dos is probably more user friendly, and the bulk of the problems with 
Well... It is user friendly because it lacks flexibility.  This is good
for novice users, since they don't have to remember so possibilities of
what they are using.  I myself hate the *user-friendliness* of the prompt
given agains "DEL *.*".  To me, it is unfriendly, since I know clearly what
I'm going to do every time I type that command.


>>    Dos is basically plug and play.
Really??  Have you been fooled around about the HIMEM, MEMMAKER, QEMM,
Windows driver problems?  Once, Windows frequently give me a GPF in USER.EXE.
I spent a lot of time to figure it out: checking if it is a harddisk problem
that causes USER.EXE to be corrupted...  I tried many many things to fix
the problem.  Finally, and accidentally, I found out the possible cause:
When I switched to another video driver for another resolution, the GPF
no long exists!  Oh!  How come a bug in the video driver causes Window
to report something wrong with USER.EXE?  (Of course, this is because
the driver is causing memory conflicts in USER.EXE)


>>    Dos users are pretty common.
UNIX users are also common.  This population is growing.


>>    Dos has a better security system. File sharing in UNIX is dangerous.
What?  Better security?
UNIX provides security measures to prevent one user from intervening another,
by password protection to prevent unauthorized access to user accounts, by
storing attributes to limit access to user (and system) files, and by 
memory protection to prevent user processes from intervening one another.

For DOS, can you prevent someone from copying your files to a floppy diskette
and then 'steal' the data away?  You may need to go for encryption softwares
for better security for data.  For UNIX, it is trivial.  You can disallow
others from accessing your own files.  That's it!  For better security,
the 'crypt' program should satisfy your normal needs.

File sharing in UNIX is dangerous?  How?
Are you worring about sharing conflicts?  Say, while you're reading from a
file, another user writes to it.  What will happen?  The designers of UNIX
have already forseen this problem and have algorithms to resolve the conflicts.
This is the functions of the SHARE.EXE that MS later adds to DOS.

File sharing is important!  It saves resources since you don't have to
duplicate the same file on every machine.  For example, for UNIX machines
connected to the same network, they can share the same C compiler, shell
interpreter, and standard UNIX command executables.  For DOS?  Unless you
go for networking, you have to keep individual copies of MSDOS and perhaps
MS-WINDOWS for each machine.  What a waste of disk space!


>>    Properly configured, Dos will run lika a champ. If you're having a 
>> problem running it in DOS, then you will probably have a problem running it
>> under any OS. The limitations are mostly Hardware.

We're now in a 32-bit computing era!  DOS is still a 16-bit OS.  Even worse,
it doesn't have good support for 32-bit programming.  Yes, DOS may be a
champ with 16-bit programs.  However, if you turn to 32-bit things, the
16-bit segment:offset memory model of DOS is useless and inefficient.
Yes, this limitation is due to HARDWARE.  It's the CPU which does not
support genuine 32-bit addressing in real mode, the mode which DOS insists
on.  For OSes like Linux, they are using the 32-bit protected mode!  It
has gone beyond the 640K, 16-bit barriers by abandoning real mode.  The
potentials of the CPU is better utilized.  Hence, better throughput is
expected.



>>    Last but not least, Dos is being replaced in 95 by a gui type OS. Wait 
>> until that comes out, and see if that doesn't better suit your needs.

Where is Chicago then?  Didn't MS said that they would release a 32-bit GUI OS
named Chicago (i.e. Windows 4.0) to replace DOS?  It was once scheduled for
July 1994.  Where is it now?  The beta release of it is still the DOS (ver
7.0) + Windows model.  

Wait?  For how long?
If I have to wait, I'd rather wait for WINE than Win 95.  WINE would enable
me to run Windows applications under UNIX/X-WINDOW.  I'm used to that 
environment: better multitasking, multi-user support, configuration for
individual user, ...


>>    By the way, what's the price on UNIX? I've never seen it in stores.
>>    Whats the minimum hardware requirement?

Linux is an implementatiof UNIX.  The *minimum* requirements are: 2MB of RAM,
a 386 or higher processor, and about 30MB of harddisk space.  This is just
a minimum.  You enjoy greater power if you have more RAM, faster processor
and more diskspace to install more programs.  Linux uses all your RAM fully,
while DOS just mainly use the 640K conventional memory.


>>    Lastly, if UNIX is soooo good, then why doesn't everybody run it.

It has been rumoured (and was true some time) that UNIX is complex and
user-unfriendly.  Nowadays, the situation has improved.  At least, the
Slackware release of Linux is quite user friendly.  Commercial UNIXes
(e.g. SCO) are even better, to attract users.




