>Surely expanding wildcards in the shell is a BAD THING ?
>
>rm proggy1.c  rm proggy2.c   shorthand rm prog*   happiness
>                               typo    rm prog *  gets expanded to wipe directory.
>
>rm cannot check, because it just gets a list of files expanded
>by the shell and does its duty.

OS/2, like Unix, has the ability to use different shells.  The
Environment variable is OS2_SHELL.  The default is CMD.EXE, which does
not expand wildcards.  CMD.EXE is no where near as good as GNU's bash
and other shells, however it is much better than the default shell
that comes with most Unices - Bourne Shell.

My personal belief is that wildcards SHOULD get expanded in the shell
for consistency, and if the port of BASH for OS/2 could run all OS/2
apps nicely I would use it as my OS2_SHELL (but, alas, it does not).
Most Unix shells which support wildcard expansion allow you to disable
it if you do not like it.


