And there are things you can do with the unix style of wildcards that DOS
can't do. Chances are though, that it is easier to work around them in unix
than in dos.
foreach i (*.doc)
cp $i $i:r.bak
end

Anyway, a much better way would be:
mkdir bak
cp *.doc bak

Much easier to handle that way.

Why not try to get dos to tell you all files with the word foo in their
name. *foo* is _NOT_ going to work. You can't have more than one * before
and after the . in a DOS wildcard (for common DOS utilities). It's
ridiculous. And you can't get around that easily.

My point is that you can find things in both that the other can't do
_exactly_ the same. Doesn't mean it's not possible. But it is probably more
likely solvable (and easier) in unix.
