>and have it do the right thing (copy a.doc to a.bak, b.doc to b.bak,
>and so forth). Because the shell handles expansion in UNIX, UNIX
>can't do this sort of thing.

Depends on your shell.  zsh allows you to turn off globbing for
selected commands.  As an example, here's a Q&D rendition of the DOS
rename command from my .zprofile:

	rename () {
		[ $# != 2 ] && {
			echo "usage: ren pat1 pat2"
			return 1
		}
		srcpat=$(echo $1 | sed -e 's/\*/\\(.*\\)/')
		dstpat=$(echo $2 | sed -e 's/\*/\\1/')
		for i in `eval echo $1` ; do
			mv -i $i $(echo $i|sed -e "s/$srcpat/$dstpat/") || break
		done
	}
	alias ren="noglob rename"

It only matches the first wildcard, doesn't handle `?', but it'll do
"ren *.c *.c.old", and you can specify a path in the destination
(ren *.doc backup/*.bak).

Rick.
