>Surely expanding wildcards in the shell is a BAD THING ?

It is often very convenient, just like these other things DOS doesn't have e.g.
filename completion, good io redirection, command history
like !jo to repeat the last command that started with jo (i.e.
jove paper.tex) or !ma instead of maple < test_file.

>rm proggy1.c  rm proggy2.c   shorthand rm prog*   happiness
>                               typo    rm prog *  gets expanded to wipe directory.

>rm cannot check, because it just gets a list of files expanded
>by the shell and does its duty.

If you are used to DOS it might be a good idea to change the default behavior
of the dangerous commands  cp, mv and rm. Include the lines
alias   mv      'mv -i'
alias   cp      'cp -i'
alias   rm      'rm -i'
in your .cshrc file (if you are using csh) or in your .profile file (if
you are using bash). You'll still be able to use the good old rm with rm -f
or /bin/rm

Good luck,
 Mark van Hoeij


