In <1995Jan2.135536.27035@imec.be> buytaert@imec.be (Steven Buytaert) writes:

>:    Dos has features that were patterned after UNIX? Name them, and tell 
>: me why you think they're corrupted. 
> OK, just to have an apetizer. I know this example has nothing to do with
> a kernel on the unix side, but let's say 'pipes' and 'wildcards' ?

Oh, foo.  I was trying to hold my finger back from the 'f' key. :-)

File descriptors.  Original MS-DOS had those funky control blocks ala CP/M.
Then they added small integer values which were received from an open()-ish
function and were passed to close/read/write/seek()-ish functions.

Hierarchical filesystem.  Original DOS had only the root filesystem.  Then
they bolted on subdirectories.  You can see this, from a SW archeological
view, if you compare the block allocation for the root to any subdirectory.

As was mentioned, pipes.  Implemented, of course, as a temp file, as DOS
can't do it the same way UNIX can.

Wildcards weren't really from UNIX.  The wildcard interface was
substantially a clone of the CP/M functionality, and is quite different from
UNIX.

I/O redirection.  Very similar to UNIX, with some strange deviations in how
such things are inherited, since they don't have UNIX's process model.

ioctl()-ish interface.  DOS started with the rather limited wasy CP/M
handled I/O operation abstraction (when it did at all).  This could arguably
be lumped with "file descriptors".

					"My recollections only"
					Andy Valencia


